DISCUSSION
5.1 Interpretation of Results
The results of the AI Enabled SOC Architecture demonstrate that deterministic algorithms, modular backend design, and explainability placeholders can effectively support SOC triage and analyst decision making. QuickSort consistently achieved extremely low latency, completing sorting operations in microseconds. This confirms its suitability for real time preprocessing of severity scores, especially in environments where thousands of events may arrive per second. The algorithm’s predictable behavior is essential in SOC workflows, where analysts must trust that triage operations are both fast and reliable.
Binary Search achieved perfect accuracy, validating its reliability for rapid lookup operations. This deterministic behavior ensures that severity values are consistently identified, reducing the likelihood of triage errors and supporting analyst confidence in the system’s outputs.
Integration testing showed that the UI, API, database, and algorithm modules communicate seamlessly. The end to end workflow—from log submission to triage output—performed consistently across multiple test cases. Acceptance testing confirmed that the system meets user expectations, while usability testing indicated strong analyst satisfaction with interface clarity, navigation, and readability of severity outputs.
Overall, the results validate the feasibility of integrating explainability and governance controls into SOC workflows. Although machine learning components were placeholders, the architecture demonstrates how XAI modules can be incorporated to enhance transparency, accountability, and analyst trust. The deterministic foundation provides stability, while the modular design supports future integration of ML based detection and advanced explainability techniques.
5.2 Comparison with Literature Findings
The findings align closely with existing literature on SOC modernization, AI enabled detection, and explainability. Research by Alshamrani et al. (2019) and Khraisat et al. (2019) highlights the limitations of rule based SIEM systems, particularly their inability to detect stealthy or multi stage attacks. The deterministic algorithms implemented in this project address part of this challenge by improving triage efficiency and reducing false positives through predictable sorting and lookup operations.
Explainability research emphasizes the importance of transparency in AI assisted security systems. Lundberg & Lee (2017) and Ribeiro et al. (2016) argue that analysts must understand why a model flagged an event before taking action. Although full SHAP and LIME implementations were not included, the architecture demonstrates how explainability modules can be integrated into SOC workflows to support analyst trust and validation.
The results of this project align with emerging SOC research showing that AI augmented workflows can significantly improve operational efficiency. Srinivas et al. (2025) found that LLM driven SOC automation improves triage accuracy, reduces analyst workload, and enhances threat intelligence extraction, but only when paired with governance safeguards that mitigate hallucinations, data leakage, and misclassification risks. These observations support the design choices in this prototype, particularly the emphasis on deterministic algorithms, transparent triage, and governed response workflows. While this project does not implement full LLM based automation, the governance and explainability mechanisms incorporated here reflect the same principles recommended for safe and trustworthy AI adoption in SOC environments.
Research on adversarial machine learning, including Biggio & Roli (2018), underscores the need for governance and human oversight. The governance layer implemented in this project aligns with these recommendations by enforcing approval gates, audit logging, and controlled response workflows. This ensures that automated actions remain accountable and traceable.
Finally, the integration of MITRE ATT&CK concepts aligns with Basel et al. (2023), who emphasize the importance of structured adversary context in SOC operations. The architecture provides a foundation for future ATT&CK mapping integration, supporting standardized reporting and improved analyst context.
5.3 Implications and Limitations
5.3.1 Implications
The project demonstrates that combining deterministic algorithms with explainability and governance controls can enhance SOC efficiency, transparency, and accountability. The modular architecture supports future integration of machine learning models, advanced XAI techniques, and automated response workflows. The system provides a practical foundation for SOC modernization and responsible AI adoption, showing how AI can assist analysts without replacing human judgment.
Key implications include:
•	Deterministic algorithms can stabilize triage workflows and reduce alert fatigue.
•	Explainability modules can improve analyst trust and support responsible AI use.
•	Governance controls ensure that automated actions remain accountable and auditable.
•	Modular design supports scalability, maintainability, and future ML integration.
•	The architecture aligns with NIST AI RMF principles for transparency, oversight, and risk management.
5.3.2 Limitations
Despite its strengths, the project has several limitations:
•	Machine learning components are placeholders and were not fully trained.
•	Simulated SOC logs do not reflect the complexity of real enterprise telemetry.
•	UI functionality is minimal and lacks advanced visualization features.
•	No integration with commercial SIEM/SOAR platforms.
•	No live SOC environment was available for testing.
•	Explainability modules (SHAP, LIME) are conceptual rather than fully implemented.
•	Governance workflows may require refinement to avoid bottlenecks or automation bias.
These limitations provide opportunities for future enhancement and real world deployment.
5.4 Future Work
Future work should focus on expanding the system’s capabilities and integrating more advanced technologies. Key recommendations include:
5.4.1 Real SOC Integration
Integrate the system with real SOC environments, SIEM platforms, and enterprise telemetry sources to validate performance under realistic conditions.
5.4.2 Advanced Machine Learning Models
Implement fully trained XGBoost, LSTM, and anomaly detection models to enhance detection accuracy and adaptability.
5.4.3 Enhanced Explainability Tools
Integrate SHAP and LIME to provide feature level explanations for ML predictions, improving analyst trust and transparency.
5.4.4 Automated Governance Workflows
Develop automated approval gates, audit logs, and response workflows aligned with responsible AI principles and organizational policies.
5.4.5 Continuous Retraining Pipelines
Implement CI/CD pipelines for model retraining, drift detection, and performance monitoring to maintain long term reliability.
5.4.6 Integration with SOAR Platforms
Connect the system to SOAR tools to automate low risk responses while preserving human oversight for high impact actions.
5.4.7 Real World SOC Dataset Validation
Although the prototype demonstrates strong integration of deterministic algorithms, explainability components, and governance controls, future work should validate the framework using real world SOC datasets. Evaluating the system against authentic enterprise telemetry, diverse attack patterns, and production scale noise would strengthen practical relevance and reveal additional optimization opportunities. Fully implemented machine learning models—such as XGBoost for structured event classification and LSTM for behavioral sequence analysis—should be incorporated to assess end to end AI performance under realistic SOC conditions. 
